1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,632
A maize root tip makes more than
seventeen thousand five hundred new cells

2
00:00:04,632 --> 00:00:05,532
every hour.

3
00:00:05,882 --> 00:00:10,450
A watermelon cell can grow up to three
lakh fifty thousand times bigger.

4
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:13,401
And a mango tree keeps
growing all its life.

5
00:00:13,851 --> 00:00:15,105
Aarav wanted numbers.

6
00:00:15,105 --> 00:00:17,986
He measured a bean seedling
with a ruler every day.

7
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:20,121
He asked: how do we measure growth?

8
00:00:20,121 --> 00:00:23,010
And does a plant grow by
the same amount each day?

9
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,039
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:25,039 --> 00:00:28,677
We measure growth as length,
area, weight or cell number.

11
00:00:28,677 --> 00:00:32,185
And the pattern can be steady,
or it can keep doubling.

12
00:00:32,685 --> 00:00:37,062
By the end, you will know the phases
of growth, arithmetic and geometric

13
00:00:37,062 --> 00:00:39,965
growth, growth rates,
and what growth needs.

14
00:00:40,614 --> 00:00:42,090
First, what is growth?

15
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:44,987
Growth is a permanent increase in size.

16
00:00:44,987 --> 00:00:46,533
It cannot be reversed.

17
00:00:46,533 --> 00:00:50,695
A balloon that fills with air and
goes flat again is not growing.

18
00:00:51,145 --> 00:00:52,879
Plants grow all their life.

19
00:00:52,879 --> 00:00:55,025
This is called indeterminate growth.

20
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:59,517
It happens because plants have meristems,
groups of cells that keep dividing.

21
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:04,445
Growth can be measured as fresh weight,
dry weight, length, area, volume

22
00:01:04,445 --> 00:01:05,553
or cell number.

23
00:01:05,553 --> 00:01:08,116
A pollen tube is measured by its length.

24
00:01:08,116 --> 00:01:10,098
A leaf, by its surface area.

25
00:01:10,748 --> 00:01:12,672
Let's zoom into a growing root.

26
00:01:13,022 --> 00:01:15,709
A seedling root gets
longer only near its tip.

27
00:01:16,209 --> 00:01:17,553
Zoom to the tip.

28
00:01:17,553 --> 00:01:22,835
Behind the root cap lie three zones, one
after another: meristematic, elongation

29
00:01:22,835 --> 00:01:24,002
and maturation.

30
00:01:24,502 --> 00:01:25,932
Zoom to the cells.

31
00:01:25,932 --> 00:01:30,929
Meristematic cells divide, with dense
cytoplasm, big nuclei and thin walls.

32
00:01:30,929 --> 00:01:34,353
Elongating cells get bigger,
with a large vacuole.

33
00:01:34,353 --> 00:01:37,993
Maturing cells reach full
size and thicken their walls.

34
00:01:38,643 --> 00:01:40,523
Now, how fast does growth happen?

35
00:01:40,523 --> 00:01:41,830
There are two patterns.

36
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:46,140
In arithmetic growth, after a cell
divides, only one daughter cell keeps

37
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:47,040
dividing.

38
00:01:46,799 --> 00:01:48,896
The other one grows up and matures.

39
00:01:49,346 --> 00:01:52,689
So the root gets longer by
the same amount each time.

40
00:01:52,689 --> 00:01:55,976
Plot length against time
and you get a straight line.

41
00:01:55,976 --> 00:01:59,413
Length at time t equals
starting length plus r times t.

42
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:03,329
In geometric growth, both
daughter cells keep dividing.

43
00:02:03,329 --> 00:02:06,358
One cell becomes two,
then four, then eight.

44
00:02:06,808 --> 00:02:08,761
At first, growth is slow.

45
00:02:08,761 --> 00:02:10,456
This is the lag phase.

46
00:02:10,456 --> 00:02:12,203
Then it shoots up fast.

47
00:02:12,203 --> 00:02:15,101
This is the log, or exponential, phase.

48
00:02:15,551 --> 00:02:18,990
But nutrients run short, so
growth slows down and stops.

49
00:02:18,990 --> 00:02:20,823
This is the stationary phase.

50
00:02:20,823 --> 00:02:25,316
The whole curve looks like the letter
S, so it is called a sigmoid curve.

51
00:02:25,766 --> 00:02:30,062
Here, final size equals starting
size times e to the power r t.

52
00:02:30,062 --> 00:02:32,441
The r is the relative growth rate.

53
00:02:32,441 --> 00:02:37,008
It shows how well a plant makes new
material, so it is also called the

54
00:02:37,008 --> 00:02:38,233
efficiency index.

55
00:02:38,883 --> 00:02:41,107
We can compare growth in two ways.

56
00:02:41,457 --> 00:02:44,831
Absolute growth rate is the
total growth per unit time.

57
00:02:45,281 --> 00:02:49,644
Relative growth rate is the growth per
unit time compared with the starting

58
00:02:49,644 --> 00:02:50,544
size.

59
00:02:50,178 --> 00:02:52,873
Small things can have a big relative rate.

60
00:02:53,523 --> 00:02:55,433
What does a plant need to grow?

61
00:02:55,783 --> 00:02:56,683
Water.

62
00:02:56,306 --> 00:02:59,086
Water makes cells swell
and press on their walls.

63
00:02:59,086 --> 00:03:01,977
This pressure, called
turgor, helps cells stretch.

64
00:03:01,977 --> 00:03:04,629
Water is also where the
enzymes do their work.

65
00:03:05,079 --> 00:03:07,370
Oxygen, to release energy from food.

66
00:03:07,370 --> 00:03:10,885
And nutrients, to build new
protoplasm and to give energy.

67
00:03:11,335 --> 00:03:15,935
Each plant also needs a temperature
range that suits it best, called its

68
00:03:15,935 --> 00:03:17,022
optimum range.

69
00:03:17,022 --> 00:03:21,195
Signals like light and gravity
also guide some stages of growth.

70
00:03:21,645 --> 00:03:22,545
Your turn.

71
00:03:22,427 --> 00:03:26,443
In a root, one daughter cell keeps
dividing while the other matures.

72
00:03:26,443 --> 00:03:28,743
Is that arithmetic or geometric growth?

73
00:03:28,743 --> 00:03:29,893
Pause and decide.

74
00:03:33,093 --> 00:03:33,993
Arithmetic!

75
00:03:33,916 --> 00:03:38,455
Only one cell keeps dividing, so the
root adds the same length each time,

76
00:03:38,455 --> 00:03:39,633
a straight line.

77
00:03:40,283 --> 00:03:41,384
Remember this.

78
00:03:41,384 --> 00:03:43,493
Arithmetic is a straight line.

79
00:03:43,493 --> 00:03:44,923
Geometric is an S.

80
00:03:45,523 --> 00:03:47,063
Now, a worked example.

81
00:03:47,063 --> 00:03:48,567
Two leaves, A and B.

82
00:03:48,917 --> 00:03:51,219
Leaf A starts at five square centimetres.

83
00:03:51,219 --> 00:03:52,658
Leaf B starts at fifty.

84
00:03:52,658 --> 00:03:56,053
In the same time, each one grows
by five square centimetres.

85
00:03:56,503 --> 00:04:00,253
So their absolute growth is the
same, five square centimetres each.

86
00:04:00,703 --> 00:04:04,815
But leaf A grew five out of five,
that is one hundred percent.

87
00:04:04,815 --> 00:04:08,072
Leaf B grew five out of
fifty, only ten percent.

88
00:04:08,072 --> 00:04:10,872
Leaf A has the higher
relative growth rate.

89
00:04:11,522 --> 00:04:12,983
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:16,628
Many students think geometric
growth keeps rising forever.

91
00:04:17,028 --> 00:04:18,854
But nutrients become limited.

92
00:04:18,854 --> 00:04:22,574
Growth slows and the curve
flattens into the stationary phase.

93
00:04:22,574 --> 00:04:25,691
That is why it is an S,
not a straight rocket.

94
00:04:26,341 --> 00:04:27,531
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:27,531 --> 00:04:31,100
As statement questions on the phases
of growth, the two growth equations

96
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:32,370
and the sigmoid curve.

97
00:04:32,970 --> 00:04:34,119
Exam level now.

98
00:04:34,119 --> 00:04:35,814
Read the four statements.

99
00:04:35,814 --> 00:04:37,012
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:42,412 --> 00:04:43,670
The answer is D.

101
00:04:43,670 --> 00:04:46,516
Statements two, three
and four are correct.

102
00:04:47,016 --> 00:04:50,890
L t equals L zero plus r t
describes arithmetic growth.

103
00:04:50,890 --> 00:04:55,726
In the geometric equation, r is the
relative growth rate, the efficiency

104
00:04:55,726 --> 00:04:56,626
index.

105
00:04:56,872 --> 00:05:00,566
The sigmoid curve has lag,
log and stationary phases.

106
00:05:00,566 --> 00:05:05,439
But in arithmetic growth only one
daughter cell keeps dividing, so statement

107
00:05:05,439 --> 00:05:06,566
one is wrong.

108
00:05:07,166 --> 00:05:08,706
Here is your recap.

109
00:05:08,706 --> 00:05:10,288
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:13,488 --> 00:05:15,674
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:16,074 --> 00:05:16,974
One.

112
00:05:16,492 --> 00:05:19,069
What are the three phases
of growth in a root?

113
00:05:19,969 --> 00:05:20,869
Two.

114
00:05:20,379 --> 00:05:22,530
What are the phases of a sigmoid curve?

115
00:05:23,430 --> 00:05:24,330
Three.

116
00:05:23,945 --> 00:05:25,851
What is the relative growth rate?

117
00:05:26,951 --> 00:05:30,628
Next time: how can a cell that stopped
dividing long ago start dividing

118
00:05:30,628 --> 00:05:31,528
again?

119
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:34,765
We find out in differentiation,
dedifferentiation and redifferentiation.
